<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="DIBOA-glucoside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6949" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Benzoxazinoids were identified in the early 1960s as secondary metabolites of the grasses (including the major agricultural crops maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565|), and rye (|FRAME: TAX-4550|)) that function as a natural defense against a wide range of pests, including insects, pathogenic fungi and bacteria |CITS:[niemeyer88]|. The benzoxazinoids also exhibit allelopathic properties |CITS:[Barnes87][10804466]|. Benzoxazinoids are synthesized in two subfamilies of the |FRAME: TAX-4479| and sporadically in single species of the dicots |CITS: [19577780]|. The most common benzoxazinoids are |FRAME: CPD-6365| and |FRAME: CPD-6366|. Since the aglycone benzoxazinoids are autotoxic, they are stored in glucosylated forms that have reduced chemical reactivity |CITS: [10804466]|. The glucosides are stored in the vacuoles |CITS:[12239364]|. This allows the plants to store the toxic compounds in anticipation of pathogen attacks, prompting the suggestion to name these compounds &quot;phytoanticipins&quot;. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The synthesis of benzoxazinoids starts with the cleavage of |FRAME: INDOLE-3-GLYCEROL-P &quot;indole-3-glycerol phosphate&quot;| (an intermediate in tryptophan biosynthesis) to form |FRAME: INDOLE|. Indole is a volatile compound and can be used, in conjunction with several terpenoids, to form cocktails of volatile products that are used in parasitic defense |CITS:[11556781]|. However, indole can be directed to the pathway described here, where is it converted into benzoxazinoids by a series of cytochrome P450-dependent monooxygenases. In successive reactions catalyzed by |FRAME: G-7861|, |FRAME: G-7862|, |FRAME: G-7863| and |FRAME: G-7864| four oxygen atoms are introduced into the indole moiety, producing |FRAME: CPD-6365|, which is glucosylated by the enzymes |FRAME: G-8201| and |FRAME: G-8202|, forming |FRAME: CPD-13811|. |FRAME: CPD-6365| is the main benzoxazinoid in barley. Some plants, like maize, continue by modifying |FRAME: CPD-13811| into |FRAME: CPD-6562|. This is described in pathway |FRAME: PWY-6950|.</Description>
